1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,980
Midnight, in the city of the caliphs. A man
sits on the executioner's mat, his eyes

2
00:00:05,980 --> 00:00:11,221
bound, a drawn sword beside him. Across the
city, an old judge wakes from his sleep,

3
00:00:11,221 --> 00:00:13,780
and cannot say why his heart is so heavy.

4
00:00:24,130 --> 00:00:29,008
Welcome to The Honest Historian. I'm Mehdi
Rahmani, and this is Folio Four. Imagine

5
00:00:29,008 --> 00:00:33,768
a man sitting on a leather mat, his eyes bound
with a cloth. Beside him stands an

6
00:00:33,768 --> 00:00:38,528
executioner with a drawn sword. And somewhere
in the dark streets of the city, an

7
00:00:38,528 --> 00:00:42,230
old judge is riding as fast as he can, without
knowing yet why.

8
00:00:43,010 --> 00:00:47,648
Today, we leave the court of Ghazni for a
while. We go back two hundred years, to

9
00:00:47,648 --> 00:00:51,770
the court of the caliphs, for a story that
Beyhaqi thought worth saving.

10
00:01:07,050 --> 00:01:11,885
First, a word about where this story sits. In
the pages of Beyhaqi just before the

11
00:01:11,885 --> 00:01:16,662
hanging of Hasanak, the vizier we met in Folio
One, Beyhaqi stops his own account

12
00:01:16,662 --> 00:01:21,438
and tells a tale from the distant past. He
says he did not invent it. He heard it

13
00:01:21,438 --> 00:01:23,030
through a chain of tellers.

14
00:01:23,310 --> 00:01:29,125
A man named Ismail ibn Shihab said that he
heard it from the judge himself, Ahmad

15
00:01:29,125 --> 00:01:30,130
ibn Abi Duwad.

16
00:01:30,910 --> 00:01:35,983
So the voice we will hear, in my retelling, is
the voice of that judge, speaking of

17
00:01:35,983 --> 00:01:37,450
a night he never forgot.

18
00:01:38,230 --> 00:01:43,062
Why does Beyhaqi tell it? He says so plainly
near the end. He writes these stories

19
00:01:43,062 --> 00:01:45,950
so that his readers may gain something from
them.

20
00:02:01,230 --> 00:02:03,790
Let me introduce the three men.

21
00:02:04,570 --> 00:02:09,149
The first is the caliph, al-Mutasim. He was
the ruler of the Abbasid empire, which

22
00:02:09,149 --> 00:02:13,784
stretched from North Africa to Central Asia.
He reigned from the year eight hundred

23
00:02:13,784 --> 00:02:18,251
and thirty three to eight hundred and forty
two. In those years his court sat at

24
00:02:18,251 --> 00:02:21,490
Samarra, a new city on the Tigris River, north
of Baghdad.

25
00:02:22,270 --> 00:02:27,234
The second is Afshin. That was a title, not a
name. It was the old title of the

26
00:02:27,234 --> 00:02:32,512
princes of Ushrusana, a mountain land in
Central Asia, near today's Tajikistan. This

27
00:02:32,512 --> 00:02:37,350
Afshin, whose own name was Khaydar, had become
the caliph's greatest general.

28
00:02:37,630 --> 00:02:42,736
He had just won a long and bitter war against
a rebel named Babak, in the mountains

29
00:02:42,736 --> 00:02:47,781
of Azerbaijan. And so, Beyhaqi tells us, the
caliph honored him above everyone. He

30
00:02:47,781 --> 00:02:49,750
was proud, powerful, and feared.

31
00:02:50,530 --> 00:02:55,844
The third is Bu Dulaf, whose full name was al-
Qasim ibn Isa al-Ijli. He was an Arab

32
00:02:55,844 --> 00:03:01,157
nobleman, a commander, a governor in western
Iran, and a famous patron of poets. He

33
00:03:01,157 --> 00:03:03,590
had served the caliphs for many years.

34
00:03:04,370 --> 00:03:09,589
And between Afshin and Bu Dulaf, there was
hatred. Beyhaqi calls it enmity and

35
00:03:09,589 --> 00:03:11,530
rivalry of the fiercest kind.

36
00:03:26,810 --> 00:03:31,325
Then there is the fourth man, the one who
tells the story. Ahmad ibn Abi Duwad was

37
00:03:31,325 --> 00:03:35,566
the chief judge of the empire. Beyhaqi tells
us he was more powerful than any

38
00:03:35,566 --> 00:03:39,861
vizier, and that he served three caliphs, one
after another. He was an Arab, a

39
00:03:39,861 --> 00:03:43,330
scholar, and a man whose word carried weight
from east to west.

40
00:03:44,110 --> 00:03:48,850
His story begins like this. One night, in the
time of al-Mutasim, he woke up at

41
00:03:48,850 --> 00:03:53,770
midnight. He could not sleep. A strange gloom
had settled on his heart, and he did

42
00:03:53,770 --> 00:03:54,550
not know why.

43
00:03:55,330 --> 00:04:00,040
He called his servant, a man named Salama, and
told him to saddle his mount. Salama

44
00:04:00,040 --> 00:04:04,467
was surprised. It is the middle of the night,
he said. And the caliph has told

45
00:04:04,467 --> 00:04:08,610
everyone that he will not receive visitors
tomorrow. Where are you going?

46
00:04:09,390 --> 00:04:14,113
The judge lay down again. But he could not
rest. So he got up, washed, dressed, and

47
00:04:14,113 --> 00:04:18,552
rode out into the dark, not even sure where he
was going. He turned toward the

48
00:04:18,552 --> 00:04:18,950
palace.

49
00:04:34,230 --> 00:04:38,827
At the palace gate, the chamberlain told him
it was far too early. The caliph was

50
00:04:38,827 --> 00:04:43,594
alone, and wished to be left alone. But the
judge insisted, and the chamberlain went

51
00:04:43,594 --> 00:04:47,510
in. He came back with a surprise. Come in, he
said. You are expected.

52
00:04:48,290 --> 00:04:52,692
The judge found al-Mutasim sitting alone, deep
in thought. And the caliph said

53
00:04:52,692 --> 00:04:57,320
something that the judge remembered all his
life. Why are you so late, he asked. I

54
00:04:57,320 --> 00:04:59,690
have been waiting for you for a long time.

55
00:05:00,470 --> 00:05:05,049
Then the caliph explained. For months, Afshin
had begged him to hand Bu Dulaf over

56
00:05:05,049 --> 00:05:09,684
to him, to take his wealth and to put him to
death. Again and again, the caliph had

57
00:05:09,684 --> 00:05:14,375
refused. Bu Dulaf was a loyal servant with a
long record. But the night before, worn

58
00:05:14,375 --> 00:05:17,390
down by Afshin's pleading, the caliph had made
a slip.

59
00:05:17,670 --> 00:05:22,418
He had given in. Worse, he had bound himself
with solemn oaths that he would not

60
00:05:22,418 --> 00:05:25,030
take Bu Dulaf back, and would not interfere.

61
00:05:25,810 --> 00:05:30,390
And now, the caliph said, as soon as day
comes, Afshin will kill him.

62
00:05:31,170 --> 00:05:35,603
The caliph could not break his oath. But
perhaps someone else could ask. Go to

63
00:05:35,603 --> 00:05:39,410
Afshin, he told the judge. Plead with him.
Save the man if you can.

64
00:05:54,690 --> 00:05:59,397
The judge rode as if his life depended on it.
In his haste, his cloak slipped from

65
00:05:59,397 --> 00:06:04,218
his shoulders and fell in the road, and he did
not even notice. He sent riders ahead

66
00:06:04,218 --> 00:06:07,490
to Bu Dulaf's house, afraid that he was
already too late.

67
00:06:08,270 --> 00:06:12,959
When he reached Afshin's house, the servants
ran to meet him, as they always did,

68
00:06:12,959 --> 00:06:17,590
for he was a great man. He told his own men to
wait at the door, and he went in.

69
00:06:18,370 --> 00:06:22,905
Here is what he saw. At the far end of the
courtyard sat Afshin. In front of him, a

70
00:06:22,905 --> 00:06:27,386
leather mat was spread on the ground. In that
world, this was the mat on which men

71
00:06:27,386 --> 00:06:31,975
were executed, so that the blood would not
stain the floor. On the mat sat Bu Dulaf,

72
00:06:31,975 --> 00:06:35,090
in nothing but his trousers, his eyes bound
with a cloth.

73
00:06:35,370 --> 00:06:40,410
Beside him stood the executioner, with his
sword drawn, waiting for one word.

74
00:06:55,690 --> 00:07:00,210
Now comes the battle of this story. And it is
not fought with swords.

75
00:07:00,990 --> 00:07:05,612
Whenever the judge visited, Afshin would rise
and bow so low that his head came to

76
00:07:05,612 --> 00:07:10,346
the judge's chest. This time, he did not move.
His face turned yellow, then red. The

77
00:07:10,346 --> 00:07:14,010
veins stood out on his neck. He would not even
look at his guest.

78
00:07:14,790 --> 00:07:19,895
The judge did not take offense. He kissed
Afshin, and sat down beside him. And then

79
00:07:19,895 --> 00:07:24,630
he began to talk. Anything, to keep Afshin's
mind away from one word: strike.

80
00:07:24,910 --> 00:07:29,331
He even praised the Persians and the peoples
of the east above his own people, the

81
00:07:29,331 --> 00:07:33,483
Arabs, because he knew this would please
Afshin, who was proud of his ancient

82
00:07:33,483 --> 00:07:38,012
princely line. Later he confessed, with some
shame, that this was a sin. But he said

83
00:07:38,012 --> 00:07:40,870
he did it to keep a man alive for a few more
minutes.

84
00:07:41,650 --> 00:07:46,710
Then he made his request. Grant me this man's
life, he said, as a favor to me.

85
00:07:47,490 --> 00:07:52,696
Afshin refused. The Commander of the Faithful
has given him to me, he said, and he

86
00:07:52,696 --> 00:07:54,410
has sworn not to interfere.

87
00:07:55,190 --> 00:07:59,802
So the judge humbled himself. He kissed
Afshin's head. He kissed his shoulder. He

88
00:07:59,802 --> 00:08:04,414
kissed his hands. He bent down toward his
knee. And Afshin, in Beyhaqi's account,

89
00:08:04,414 --> 00:08:07,830
told him coldly that a thousand kisses would
do him no good.

90
00:08:23,110 --> 00:08:27,170
And here the judge made a choice that could
have cost him everything.

91
00:08:27,950 --> 00:08:32,602
He drew himself up and reminded Afshin who he
was. Then he said that he carried a

92
00:08:32,602 --> 00:08:37,369
message from the caliph himself. And the
message, as Beyhaqi gives it, was this. Do

93
00:08:37,369 --> 00:08:41,963
not kill al-Qasim. Do not touch him. Send him
home at once, because your hand is

94
00:08:41,963 --> 00:08:42,710
short of him.

95
00:08:42,990 --> 00:08:46,790
And if you kill him, I will have you put to
death in his place.

96
00:08:47,570 --> 00:08:52,442
There was no such message. The caliph had said
nothing of the kind. The judge had

97
00:08:52,442 --> 00:08:55,570
just invented it, on the spot, in the caliph's
name.

98
00:08:56,350 --> 00:09:01,408
Afshin trembled. He went pale, and almost
fainted. Is this truly the caliph's

99
00:09:01,408 --> 00:09:02,590
message, he asked.

100
00:09:03,370 --> 00:09:07,898
The judge did not answer with words alone. He
called in thirty or forty respected

101
00:09:07,898 --> 00:09:12,539
men, trusted witnesses from every quarter, and
delivered the message again in front

102
00:09:12,539 --> 00:09:17,123
of them. Then he turned to the man on the mat
and asked him, are you well? Are you

103
00:09:17,123 --> 00:09:17,570
wounded?

104
00:09:17,850 --> 00:09:23,273
And from behind the blindfold Bu Dulaf
answered that he was unhurt. The witnesses

105
00:09:23,273 --> 00:09:24,010
saw it all.

106
00:09:24,790 --> 00:09:29,590
Then the judge mounted and rode back to the
palace, as fast as he had come.

107
00:09:44,870 --> 00:09:49,545
He arrived sweating and out of breath. The
caliph had a servant wipe his face, and

108
00:09:49,545 --> 00:09:53,650
asked him what had happened. And the judge
began to tell him everything.

109
00:09:54,430 --> 00:09:59,347
He had just reached the part about the
thousand kisses when the door opened, and in

110
00:09:59,347 --> 00:10:00,650
walked Afshin himself.

111
00:10:01,430 --> 00:10:05,981
Think about the judge's fear in that moment.
If Afshin told the caliph about the

112
00:10:05,981 --> 00:10:10,702
message, and the caliph denied it, then the
judge would be shamed as a liar, and Bu

113
00:10:10,702 --> 00:10:12,010
Dulaf would surely die.

114
00:10:12,790 --> 00:10:17,960
Afshin spoke. Ahmad brought me a message from
you, he said. Is it true that I may

115
00:10:17,960 --> 00:10:18,790
not kill him?

116
00:10:19,570 --> 00:10:22,730
And the caliph answered. It is my message.

117
00:10:23,510 --> 00:10:28,001
Then al-Mutasim reproached his general. Bu
Dulaf was a servant of this house, he

118
00:10:28,001 --> 00:10:32,661
said. The better path would have been to set
him free with honor. And then he added

119
00:10:32,661 --> 00:10:34,850
a line that is still quoted in Persian.

120
00:10:35,630 --> 00:10:40,150
Har kasi aan konad ke az asl o gowhar-e vey
sazad.

121
00:10:40,930 --> 00:10:44,710
Everyone does what befits his root and his
nature.

122
00:10:45,490 --> 00:10:47,750
Afshin left, defeated.

123
00:11:03,030 --> 00:11:07,888
When they were alone, the caliph turned to the
judge. Why, he asked, did you deliver

124
00:11:07,888 --> 00:11:09,450
a message I never gave you?

125
00:11:10,230 --> 00:11:14,756
The judge answered honestly. He said he could
not bear to see a Muslim's blood

126
00:11:14,756 --> 00:11:19,456
spilled. He would accept whatever punishment
came. And he believed that God would

127
00:11:19,456 --> 00:11:22,590
not hold him to account for a lie told to save
a life.

128
00:11:23,370 --> 00:11:28,204
And the caliph laughed. He approved of what
the judge had done. Then he sent one of

129
00:11:28,204 --> 00:11:32,921
his own officers, with his own horse, to bring
Bu Dulaf with honor to the judge's

130
00:11:32,921 --> 00:11:33,270
house.

131
00:11:34,050 --> 00:11:38,927
When the judge came home, Bu Dulaf was waiting
at his door. He threw himself at the

132
00:11:38,927 --> 00:11:43,804
judge's feet. The judge raised him up,
embraced him, and told him to thank God, and

133
00:11:43,804 --> 00:11:44,450
the caliph.

134
00:11:59,730 --> 00:12:03,933
Beyhaqi ends the tale simply. Anyone who reads
this story, he writes, will

135
00:12:03,933 --> 00:12:08,704
understand what great men these were. They
have all passed away, and what remains of

136
00:12:08,704 --> 00:12:13,418
them is their good name. And then, almost
briskly, he says that he will now go back

137
00:12:13,418 --> 00:12:14,270
to his history.

138
00:12:15,050 --> 00:12:19,813
Beyhaqi does not tell us what happened to
Afshin. But other historians do. Not long

139
00:12:19,813 --> 00:12:24,576
after, the great general fell from favor. He
was arrested, put on trial in front of

140
00:12:24,576 --> 00:12:29,224
the court, and accused of disloyalty and of
secretly holding to the old faiths of

141
00:12:29,224 --> 00:12:29,970
his homeland.

142
00:12:30,250 --> 00:12:35,508
He died in prison, in the year eight hundred
and forty one. Fortune, once again, had

143
00:12:35,508 --> 00:12:36,510
turned its face.

144
00:12:51,790 --> 00:12:56,592
Why did Beyhaqi place this story here, just
before the hanging of Hasanak? He does

145
00:12:56,592 --> 00:13:01,102
not say directly, so I will not put words in
his mouth. But I notice this. In

146
00:13:01,102 --> 00:13:05,846
Hasanak's story, a man of power used his
position to destroy an enemy, and nobody

147
00:13:05,846 --> 00:13:06,490
stopped it.

148
00:13:06,770 --> 00:13:12,206
In this story, a man of power used his
position to save a life, at great risk to

149
00:13:12,206 --> 00:13:12,750
himself.

150
00:13:13,530 --> 00:13:18,529
Beyhaqi lived among courtiers. He knew how
easy it was to stay silent. I think he

151
00:13:18,529 --> 00:13:22,170
wanted his readers to see that it is never the
only choice.

152
00:13:22,950 --> 00:13:27,963
If there is one lesson here, it is this: a
single brave word, at the right moment,

153
00:13:27,963 --> 00:13:30,470
can be worth more than a thousand kisses.

154
00:13:45,750 --> 00:13:50,937
Next week, we come back to Ghazni, to meet the
man who taught Beyhaqi everything he

155
00:13:50,937 --> 00:13:55,750
knew. Bu Nasr Moshkan was the master of the
royal chancery, and he knew how a

156
00:13:55,750 --> 00:13:58,250
secretary survives at a dangerous court.

157
00:13:59,030 --> 00:14:03,778
If you enjoyed this story, follow the show so
you don't miss the next one, and share

158
00:14:03,778 --> 00:14:08,414
it with someone who loves history. I'm Mehdi
Rahmani. This episode was retold from

159
00:14:08,414 --> 00:14:13,162
the Persian original of the History of
Beyhaqi, and narrated with an AI rendering of

160
00:14:13,162 --> 00:14:15,310
my own voice. Thank you for listening.

